<body>
<h1>Unit 3: Kinematics</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Mechanics</p><p class="summary">Kinematics describes how things move without saying why. Its power is the graph: a position-time graph carries velocity, acceleration and everything else inside it, and the constant-acceleration formulas are only a special case of what the graph can show.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>Kinematics is the description of motion; dynamics is the explanation of motion. Newton's laws belong to the second half of the story.</li><li>v = u + at, s = ut + (1/2)at^2 and v^2 = u^2 + 2as all assume constant acceleration over the interval considered.</li><li>Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity, not of speed: a body moving in a circle at constant speed is accelerating continuously.</li><li>On a position-time graph the slope is velocity, the curvature is acceleration; the area under a velocity-time graph is displacement.</li><li>Near the Earth's surface a body in free fall accelerates at g = 9.8 m/s^2 downward, and the independence from mass holds only when air resistance is negligible.</li><li>The choice of sign convention (up positive or down positive) is a free decision; the physics is unchanged, but the arithmetic changes if you are inconsistent.</li><li>The three equations of motion are not three independent laws: they are one kinematic constraint written three ways, and they hold only for constant acceleration.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>The constant-acceleration formulas fail for air drag, terminal velocity, appreciable changes in g with altitude, and charged particles in fields.</li><li>A vertical throw and a free fall from rest have identical acceleration-time graphs even though their velocity graphs differ - a standard examination trap.</li><li>Average velocity over a closed round trip is zero while the average speed is not, because the average of a vector quantity is not the average of its magnitude.</li><li>Projectile motion ignores the Earth's curvature and rotation; those corrections matter for long-range artillery and for orbital projectiles, not for a 10 m throw.</li><li>Each equation of motion throws away the shape of the velocity-time graph, so two motions with the same initial and final velocity can still be different - the acceleration history is what gets lost.</li></ul><h2>Exam coverage — 5 NEB topics</h2><ul><li>Instantaneous velocity and acceleration</li><li>Relative velocity</li><li>Equation of motion (graphical treatment)</li><li>Motion of a freely falling body</li><li>Projectile motion and its applications</li></ul><h2>Common misconceptions — 3 to unlearn</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>“A body at the top of its vertical throw has zero velocity, so it has zero acceleration.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> Velocity and acceleration are different quantities. At the top the velocity is momentarily zero, but the acceleration is still g downwards - that is exactly what turns the upward motion into the downward motion.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity. The velocity changes from +u to -u through zero, so the acceleration at the top is g = 9.8 m/s^2 downwards, not zero.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“The three equations of motion are three separate laws of physics.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> They are not independent and they are not laws of nature; they are algebraic consequences of the definitions of velocity and acceleration, and they hold only for motion with constant acceleration along a straight line.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> v = u + at, s = ut + (1/2) a t^2 and v^2 = u^2 + 2 a s are one relation written in three ways. Each requires constant a and one-dimensional motion; any other motion must be handled in pieces or by calculus.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“Two bodies thrown horizontally from the same height hit the ground at the same time, because they fall the same distance.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> The fall time depends on the vertical motion only, and the vertical motion is identical for both - but the speed with which each body covers the horizontal distance is different, so they land at different places.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> t = sqrt(2 h / g) is the same for both bodies, while the horizontal ranges are R = u_x t and differ with the horizontal launch speed. What differs is the range, not the time.</p></div><h2>Workbook — 3 questions with answers</h2><div class="item"><p class="status">numerical</p><p><b>A car starts from rest and accelerates uniformly at 2.0 m/s^2 for 5.0 s on a level road. Find its final speed and the distance covered.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> v = u + a t = 0 + 2.0 x 5.0 = 10 m/s. s = u t + (1/2) a t^2 = 0 + (1/2)(2.0)(5.0^2) = 25 m. (Cross-check with v^2 = u^2 + 2 a s: 100 = 2 x 2.0 x s, so s = 25 m.)</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">derivation</p><p><b>Starting from v = u + a t and s = u t + (1/2) a t^2, derive the third equation of motion v^2 = u^2 + 2 a s.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> From v = u + a t, t = (v - u)/a. Substituting into s = u t + (1/2) a t^2 gives s = u (v - u)/a + (1/2) a (v - u)^2 / a^2 = (v - u)/a [u + (1/2)(v - u)] = (v - u)(v + u) / (2 a) = (v^2 - u^2) / (2 a). Multiplying by 2 a gives v^2 - u^2 = 2 a s, i.e. v^2 = u^2 + 2 a s. The step requires a != 0.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">reasoning</p><p><b>A stone is dropped from rest from a 45 m cliff. Find the time of fall and the speed on landing, and state the assumptions your answer depends on.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> Taking g = 9.8 m/s^2: t = sqrt(2 h / g) = sqrt(2 x 45 / 9.8) = sqrt(9.18) = 3.03 s; v = g t = 9.8 x 3.03 = 29.7 m/s. The assumptions are: a uniform gravitational field (valid over 45 m), no air resistance, the stone treated as a point, and the cliff top taken as the datum. The last assumption is what makes the speed 29.7 m/s and not 30.6 m/s measured from the base.</p></div><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Standard gravity</td><td>9.80665 m/s^2</td></tr><tr><td>Typical terminal speed of a skydiver</td><td>about 55 m/s</td></tr><tr><td>Speed of sound in air at 20 degrees C</td><td>about 343 m/s</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>How precisely can the equivalence of free fall be tested as the mass of the test body is reduced?</b></p><p class="status">Under active scrutiny</p><p>Drop-tower and lunar/planetary drop experiments push the test to ever smaller masses; any quantum-gravity signal would appear as a deviation from the predicted free-fall trajectory.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>Does a trajectory carry more information than any observer can extract from it, given that measuring position disturbs the motion?</b></p><p class="status">Open question</p><p>Quantum limits on phase-space resolution remain an active research topic, and the classical description hides the disturbance entirely.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Derive v^2 = u^2 + 2as from the definition of average velocity, stating at each step where constant acceleration was used.</li><li>Sketch x-t, v-t and a-t graphs for a ball thrown vertically upward and mark every sign change.</li><li>A car travelling at 20 m/s brakes to rest in 2.5 s: find the retardation, then the reaction distance needed to avoid a collision.</li></ol><h2>Next steps on this platform</h2><ul><li><a href="/class-11-notes/physics/chapters/kinematics">Read the chapter notes: Kinematics</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-th-kinematics">Kinematics theory lab</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-calc-projectile">Projectile motion calculator</a></li></ul><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/preface">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 1</a></li><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/dark-matter/">NASA: Dark Matter</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/3-4-motion-with-constant-acceleration">OpenStax: Motion with Constant Acceleration</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/3-5-free-fall">OpenStax: Free Fall</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
</body>
